//! The MINIMAL assistant session: provision a workspace, run ONE agent round,
//! return the reply. No loop, no operator signal, no round budget — those are
//! the full session's job (`assistant.awl`), and this document exists to show
//! the agent seam with nothing else in the frame.
//!
//! It began as a spike proving that an AWL document could drive the agent-dev
//! worker end to end, which it did, on a real server. It is kept — and kept in
//! agreement — for a reason that is operational rather than sentimental:
//!
//!   THIS DOCUMENT SHARES QUEUE `default` WITH `assistant.awl`, AND THE TWO
//!   MUST DECLARE THE SHARED ACTIONS IDENTICALLY.
//!
//! Worker contract admission holds a registering worker against EVERY
//! reachable contract on its queue, and input compatibility is contravariant.
//! When this document declared `assistant_provision(repo_path, run_id)` and
//! the full session declared it with `objective` as well, the worker could
//! satisfy only one of them — so it was refused outright and NEITHER document
//! could run. That took queue `default` down.
//!
//! A deployed workflow type cannot be removed from a running server (a
//! route-active version refuses to unload, and there is no operation that
//! clears the route), so this type is served for as long as the server lives.
//! Deleting the source would leave a deployed workflow with no source at all.
//! It is therefore maintained, and
//! `examples/agent-dev/worker/src/descriptors_tests.rs` asserts the agreement
//! so a future edit to either document cannot quietly break the queue again.
//!
//! It also exists because the Gleam-authored `assistant` package cannot start
//! at all under queue-routed serving (docs/design/aion-authoring/
//! GLEAM-PATH-CANNOT-START-FINDING.md): a project manifest has no field for a
//! task queue, so every activity is committed unscoped and start is refused.
//! AWL declares its worker queue, so it is the only surface that can.
workflow assistant_spike
  timeout 1h
  input objective: String
  input repo_path: String
  outcome answered: type Answered, route success

/// The provisioned session workspace, materialised at `<root>/<run_id>/repo`,
/// and the composed round-one prompt. Declared exactly as `assistant.awl`
/// declares it — see the module note on shared-queue agreement.
type Workspace { path: String, opening_prompt: String }

/// What one proven round returns: the agent's reply and where it worked.
type Answered { reply: String, workspace_path: String }

worker default
  /// Materialises the session workspace and composes the round-one prompt.
  /// `run_id` must be the workflow's own run id: it keys the worker-side
  /// directory AND must equal the id the agent-harness `--workspace-root`
  /// template expands to, or the agent works somewhere other than where it was
  /// provisioned. The signature is IDENTICAL to `assistant.awl`'s by
  /// requirement, not by coincidence.
  action assistant_provision(repo_path: String, run_id: String, objective: String) -> Workspace

  /// One agent round under the norn-harness contract: ONE prompt string in,
  /// ONE terminal-text string out. The worker pins one norn session per
  /// activity type per run, so repeated dispatches resume one conversation.
  action assistant(prompt: String) -> String

step provision
  assistant_provision(repo_path: repo_path, run_id: workflow.id, objective: objective) -> session

step round after provision
  // The prompt is the one the worker composed: the full working contract plus
  // the operator's objective. Sending the bare objective instead would open
  // the session with no contract at all.
  assistant(prompt: session.opening_prompt) -> reply
  route answered(reply: reply, workspace_path: session.path)
